WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:07.406
Albert Einstein is by far the best-known scientist of the twentieth century. His name and image are symbols of genius.

2
00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:16.281
Einstein's scientific contributions changed the way people understand the workings of the universe and will continue to impact the world for years to come.

3
00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:21.824
It all started with his curiosity and his belief that "imagination is more important than knowledge."

4
00:00:21.825 --> 00:00:29.612
Born in 1879 in Ulm, Germany, Albert was the son of middle-class Jewish parents, Hermann and Pauline Einstein.

5
00:00:29.613 --> 00:00:35.381
His abilities were not immediately obvious; he was even slow to start speaking as a young boy.

6
00:00:35.382 --> 00:00:44.255
During his early education, many of his teachers considered him a lazy dreamer because he did not like the rote learning methods used in his German school.

7
00:00:44.256 --> 00:00:48.817
But he had a talent for numbers and a strong interest in the sciences.

8
00:00:48.818 --> 00:00:55.928
Einstein's father and uncle started a business making electrical devices, such as motors, which fascinated Albert.

9
00:00:55.929 --> 00:01:00.917
When his father showed him a compass, Albert was amazed that the needle always pointed north.

10
00:01:00.918 --> 00:01:05.643
He wanted to know more about the unseen forces called "electricity" and "magnetism."

11
00:01:05.644 --> 00:01:14.795
His uncle Jacob was a powerful influence on young Einstein, teaching him to use his imagination to come up with explanations for the phenomena around him.

12
00:01:14.796 --> 00:01:24.449
To help the child solve math problems and understand scientific theories, Jacob would use examples from everyday life to make difficult concepts understandable.

13
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.402
Einstein loved the idea that mathematical formulas could describe reality.

14
00:01:29.403 --> 00:01:33.061
When Einstein was 15, his family left Germany.

15
00:01:33.062 --> 00:01:38.268
He attended high school in Switzerland, at a school where he was encouraged to explore his interests.

16
00:01:38.269 --> 00:01:45.435
It was during this time that Einstein first visualized traveling alongside a beam of light and wrote his first scientific paper.

17
00:01:45.436 --> 00:01:53.761
After completing high school, Einstein enrolled at a respected university in Zurich, now known as the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology.

18
00:01:53.762 --> 00:02:00.148
He angered some of his professors by frequently cutting classes and using one of his classmates' lecture notes.

19
00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:07.861
And without positive letters of recommendation from his instructors, Einstein could not get a job when he graduated in 1900.

20
00:02:07.862 --> 00:02:13.353
Finally, in 1902, he got a job at the Swiss Patent Office in Bern through a friend.

21
00:02:13.354 --> 00:02:19.739
Einstein spent his days examining applications for patents on new inventions and deducing whether a device would work.

22
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:24.025
The job was enjoyable and allowed him to refine his own scientific theories.

23
00:02:24.026 --> 00:02:28.947
But since he did not have access to a laboratory, he performed "thought experiments."

24
00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:35.176
In these "experiments," he imagined a situation and reasoned through the processes that could affect its outcome.

25
00:02:35.177 --> 00:02:43.214
He would jot down ideas on sheets of paper he kept in his desk drawer. He joked that the drawer was his department of theoretical physics.

26
00:02:43.215 --> 00:02:48.993
In 1905, Einstein returned to his thought experiment of riding a beam of light.

27
00:02:48.994 --> 00:02:57.317
He imagined riding in a streetcar at the speed of light—around 300,000 kilometers per second—away from the clock tower in Bern.

28
00:02:57.318 --> 00:03:03.594
If he looked back at the clock, it would appear to have stopped, but the watch in his pocket would function normally.

29
00:03:03.595 --> 00:03:08.028
Einstein later described this revelation as "a storm" that "broke loose in my mind."

30
00:03:08.029 --> 00:03:14.304
He had realized that if something travels at a speed close to that of light, then the flow of time can appear to change.

31
00:03:14.305 --> 00:03:20.699
So, if a ship were traveling at such a speed, time on the ship would appear to slow down to someone standing still.

32
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:30.471
Scientists proved this concept in 1971 by sending a jet around the world at six hundred miles per hour, with an extremely accurate clock onboard.

33
00:03:30.472 --> 00:03:35.684
When the jet landed, the clock was slightly behind an identical clock sitting still on earth.

34
00:03:35.685 --> 00:03:39.954
Einstein’s realization about time marked the beginning of his rise.

35
00:03:39.955 --> 00:03:45.218
Scientific thinkers up until this time had thought that time, space, and matter were all fixed.

36
00:03:45.219 --> 00:03:52.282
Einstein's theory led him to ask: if time and space could be affected by speed, why not matter and energy?

37
00:03:52.283 --> 00:04:01.100
In response, he devised the famous equation E = mc2 (where E is energy, m is mass, and c is the speed of light).

38
00:04:01.101 --> 00:04:07.584
According to the formula, since the speed of light is constant, mass and energy must be interchangeable.

39
00:04:07.585 --> 00:04:14.871
In the fall of 1905, he published an astounding scientific article introducing his "theory of special relativity."

40
00:04:14.872 --> 00:04:23.979
That same year, Einstein received his doctoral degree and published a number of other groundbreaking articles. He was 26 years old.

41
00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:32.117
Over the following years, Einstein's ideas slowly came to the attention of the scientific community, and he became increasingly respected.

42
00:04:32.118 --> 00:04:39.463
In 1914, Einstein became a professor of theoretical physics at the University of Berlin in Germany.

43
00:04:39.464 --> 00:04:43.849
One question filled his mind—what could explain the force of gravity?

44
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:51.777
In 1915, he finalized his "theory of general relativity," stating that gravity can be explained as a bending of space.

45
00:04:51.778 --> 00:04:56.658
One way to imagine this effect would be to think of a bowling ball placed on a bed.

46
00:04:56.659 --> 00:05:01.671
The bowling ball is heavy, so it will sink a little bit, creating a dip in the bed.

47
00:05:01.672 --> 00:05:08.058
If a smaller ball is then placed on the same bed, it will roll down into the dip, toward the bowling ball.

48
00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:17.370
Einstein theorized that gravity pulls objects toward the earth because space bends—just as the bed’s surface bends under the bowling ball.

49
00:05:17.371 --> 00:05:24.370
The theory predicted that gravity would affect light in the same way, forcing it to bend around objects with a large mass.

50
00:05:24.371 --> 00:05:31.645
In 1919, astronomers studying a solar eclipse announced that their measurements supported Einstein's theory.

51
00:05:31.646 --> 00:05:37.987
He became an instant celebrity and, in 1921, was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics.

52
00:05:37.988 --> 00:05:46.567
In 1932, Adolf Hitler and the Nazi party were coming into power in Germany, and Jewish intellectuals were in serious danger.

53
00:05:46.568 --> 00:05:51.073
Einstein fled to the United States, settling in Princeton, New Jersey.

54
00:05:51.074 --> 00:06:02.108
At the Institute for Advanced Study, he began looking for a mathematical explanation that would describe all the forces of nature, a concept he called "unified field theory."

55
00:06:02.109 --> 00:06:13.167
By this time, physicists had confirmed Einstein's prediction that mass could be converted to energy, and many were concerned that this technology would be used to create a weapon.

56
00:06:13.168 --> 00:06:22.764
At the start of World War II in Europe, Einstein signed a famous letter to U.S. President Franklin D. Roosevelt, warning that Germany could be building an atom bomb.

57
00:06:22.765 --> 00:06:31.821
Although Einstein was a pacifist, he was concerned about Germany’s aggressive tactics and believed that the United States must develop the weapon first.

58
00:06:31.822 --> 00:06:44.692
After the Americans devastated two Japanese cities with nuclear explosives, Albert Einstein returned to his normally pacifist views, speaking out against weapons of mass destruction and war in general.

59
00:06:44.693 --> 00:06:54.337
Einstein spent the next years of his life working with antiwar groups, giving speeches on the need for civil rights, and expanding his unified field theory.

60
00:06:54.338 --> 00:07:02.004
The creative genius died of heart failure on April 18, 1955, before he could fully develop the theory.

61
00:07:02.005 --> 00:07:11.005
Nevertheless, Einstein's ideas continue to serve as the basis for modern research and to be an inspiration for scientists all over the world.

62
00:07:11.006 --> 00:07:17.084
His curiosity and imagination truly changed the way people understand the universe.

